Here is a summary with some things left out.
<details><summary>sketch</summary>By Pick's Theorem, the area of a polygon with lattice point vertices is \(I+B/2-1\) where \(I\) is the number of points on the interior and \(B\) is the number of points on the boundary. Since we want to minimize the total sum, we can minimize the \(I+B/2\)-"cost" of each polygon by shriveling them down to contain as few points as possible. We also want to have lots of polygons so the \(-1\) can have a greater effect. Thus, the optimal setup has either \(\frac{1001^2-4}3\) isosceles right triangles with leg length \(1\) and \(1\) square with side length \(1\) or \(\frac{1001^2+2}3\) isosceles right triangles with leg length \(1.\)

It suffices to prove that such a setup exists, and for this, it suffices to prove that a \(1001 \times 1001\) grid can be tiled with all three-square L-trominoes except for one unit square. Here is a tiling for \(5 \times 5,\) given that \(2 \times 3\) rectangles are trivially tiled. [asy]
draw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(0,5)--cycle);
draw((2,0)--(2,3));
draw((5,2)--(2,2));
draw((3,5)--(3,2));
draw((0,3)--(3,3));
[/asy]
From here we can use induction on 5 mod 6 or 5 mod 11 to get 1001.</details>

