Let  $D \in \ell$  be the exsimilicenter of  $c_1$  and  $c_2$ , and let the common tangent of  $c_1$  and  $c_2$  passing through  $A$  intersect  $\ell$  at  $E$ . Let  $X_1$  and  $X_2$  be the touchpoints of  $\ell$  with  $c_1$  and  $c_2$ , respectively. It’s easy to see that  $O_1E \perp O_2E$  since both  $O_1E$  and  $O_2E$  bisect  $\angle AEX_1$  and  $\angle AEX_2$ . Moreover,  $\angle O_1EX_1 = \angle AEX_1 / 2 = \angle AO_2X_2 / 2 = \angle AO_2E$ . Therefore, the circle with diameter  $O_1O_2$  touches  $\ell$  at  $E$ . Call this circle  $c_3$ . Since the center of  $c_3$  is the midpoint of  $O_1O_2$ , and its radius is the average of the radii of  $c_1$  and  $c_2$ , it’s also homothetic to  $c_1$  and  $c_2$  with center  $D$ . Let  $F$  be the antipode of  $E$  on  $c_3$ , and let  $DE$  intersect  $c_3$  for the second time at  $G$ . 

Let  $O$  be the center of  $c$  and  $L$  be the radical center of  $c$ ,  $c_1$ , and  $c_2$ . Since the three circles are pairwise tangent to each other, so  $LA = LB = LC$ . Therefore,  $L \in O_1U$  and  $L \in O_2V \implies L = O_1U \cap O_2V$ . Also,  $L \in EA$ , the common tangent of  $c_1$  and  $c_2$ . Let  $Z$  be the midpoint of  $ED$ . Note that
\[ -1 = (O_1, O_2; A, D) \stackrel{L}{=} (U, V; E, D), \]
implying  $ZE^2 = ZD^2 = ZU \times ZV$ . Since  $EG \perp GD$  and  $Z$  is the midpoint of  $ED$ , we have  $ZG$  is tangent to  $c_3$  and also  $ZG^2 = ZE^2 = ZD^2$ . Therefore, 
\[ ZG^2 = ZU \times ZV, \]
implying  $ZG$  is also tangent to  $\odot(VGU)$  at  $G$ .

We are left to prove that  $UG \perp GV$ . We will do length chasing. By the property of harmonic bundle  $(U, V; E, D)$ , it suffices to show that  $UG$  bisect  $\angle EGD$ , or  $EG/GD = EU/UD$ . Note that  $EG/GD = EF/ED$ , which equals the ratio of the diameter of the circle to the length of the tangent line from  $D$  to the circle, along  $\ell$ . On the other hand, from menelaus we have:
\begin{align*}
\frac{EU}{UD} \cdot \frac{DO_1}{O_1A} \cdot \frac{AL}{LE} &= 1 
\implies \frac{EU}{UD} &= \frac{O_1A}{DO_1} \cdot \frac{LE}{AL}.
\end{align*}
Let  $a$ ,  $b$ ,  $c$  be the radius of  $c_1$ ,  $c_2$ , and  $c$ , respectively (WLOG  $a>b$ ). It’s known that  $X_1X_2 = 2\sqrt{ab}$ , and furthermore  $X_1X_2$  also equals the total length of external common tangent of  $c, c_1$  and  $c, c_2$ , which is  $2\sqrt{ac} + 2\sqrt{ab}$ . So,  $2\sqrt{ab} = 2\sqrt{ac} + 2\sqrt{bc}$ , implying  $c = ab/(a+b+2\sqrt{ab})$ . We have  $AE = \sqrt{AO_1 \cdot AO_2} = \sqrt{ab}$ , and  $AL$  is the inradius of  $\triangle OO_1O_2$  with sides  $a+b$ ,  $a+c$ ,  $b+c$ , which is known to be  $\sqrt{abc/(a+b+c)}$  by Heron.

The right triangle  $O_1X_1D$  has sides ratio of  $(a-b) : 2\sqrt{ab} : (a+b)$ , and from here we easily obtain  $DO_1 = a(a+b)/(a-b)$  and  $DX_1 = 2\sqrt{ab}a/(a-b)$ . Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\frac{EG}{GD} = \frac{EU}{UD} &\iff \frac{2a}{\frac{2\sqrt{ab}a}{(a-b)}} = \frac{a}{\frac{a(a+b)}{(a-b)}} \cdot \frac{AE - AL}{AL} 
&\iff \frac{a+b+\sqrt{ab}}{\sqrt{ab}} = \frac{AE}{AL} = \frac{\sqrt{ab}}{\sqrt{\frac{abc}{(a+b+c)}}} 
&\iff \frac{a+b}{\sqrt{ab}} + 1 = \sqrt{\frac{(a+b+c)}{c}} 
&\iff \frac{(a+b)^2}{ab} + \frac{2(a+b)}{\sqrt{ab}} = \frac{(a+b)}{c} 
&\iff \frac{(a+b)}{ab} + \frac{2\sqrt{ab}}{ab} = \frac{a+b+2\sqrt{ab}}{ab} = \frac{1}{c},
\end{align*}
which is true.  $\square$

OK, really nice problem. Classical complete quadrilateral geometry setup. Let  $M$  be the Miquel Point of quadrilateral  $ABCD$ . Let  $R$  be the foot of the perpendicular from  $P$  to  $QK$ . Let  $O$  be the center of  $\omega$ .  Finally, let  $S$  be the intersection of  $PF$  with  $AD$ . Attached, is the diagram we shall be referring to.
[asy]
import geometry;
size(10cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(9pt));

pen pri; pri=RGB(24, 105, 174);
pen sec; sec=RGB(217, 165, 179);
pen tri; tri=RGB(126, 123, 235);
pen fil=invisible;
pen sfil=invisible;
pen tfil=invisible;

pair A,B,C,D,E,F,P,Q,K,T,S,R,J,L,M;
pair A = dir(70);
pair B = dir(210);
pair C = dir(330);
pair D = dir(20);
pair E = midpoint(A--D);
pair F = midpoint (B--C);
pair P = intersectionpoint(line(A,B),line(C,D));
pair Q = intersectionpoint(line(A,D),line(B,C));
pair K = intersectionpoint(line(A,C),line(B,D));
pair T = intersectionpoint(line(P,E),line(B,C));
pair S = intersectionpoint(line(P,F),line(A,D));
pair L = intersectionpoint(line(Q,K),line(E,F));
pair R = intersectionpoints(circle(F,T,L),line(Q,K))[1];
pair M = intersectionpoints(circle(A,P,D),circle(A,B,Q))[0];
pair J = intersectionpoints(circle(P,K,M),circle(R,L,T))[0];

filldraw((path)(A--B--C--D--cycle), white+0.1*pri, pri);
filldraw(circumcircle(A,B,C), tfil, tri);
draw(A--P);
draw(D--Q);
draw(D--P);
draw(C--Q);
draw(A--C);
draw(B--D);
draw(P--T);
draw(P--F);
draw(P--Q);
filldraw(circumcircle(R,L,T), tfil, sec+dashed);
filldraw(circumcircle(P,K,M), tfil, pri);

dot(" $A$ ",A,dir(60));
dot(" $B$ ",B,dir(260));
dot(" $C$ ",C,dir(260));
dot(" $D$ ",D,dir(260));
dot(" $E$ ",E,dir(260));
dot(" $F$ ",F,dir(260));
dot(" $P$ ",P,dir(90));
dot(" $Q$ ",Q,dir(260));
dot(" $K$ ",K,dir(70));
dot(" $T$ ",T,dir(270));
dot(" $S$ ",S,dir(180));
dot(" $L$ ",L,dir(120));
dot(" $R$ ",R,dir(110));
dot(" $M$ ",M,dir(110));
dot(" $J$ ",J,dir(50));
[/asy]
We immediately have from the definition that  $R$  lies on  $(PK)$  and thus  $PRKM$  must be cyclic, since its well known that  $KM \perp PQ$ . Now, note that  $J$  must also lie on this circle since we are given that  $KJ \perp PT$ . Now, we make our first major observation. 

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim :**Quadrilateral  $RFTJ$  is cyclic.</span>
*Proof :* We simply note that since by Brocard's Theorem,  $PO \perp QK$ , so  $P-R-O$ . Now, this means  $OR \perp RK$ , so  $R$  must lie on the circle with diameter  $OK$ . It is well known that this circle passes through all of  $E$  ,  $F$  and  $M$ . Now, we have
\[\measuredangle TJR = \measuredangle PJR = \measuredangle PMR = \measuredangle QFR = \measuredangle TFR\]
from which it is clear that  $RFTJ$  is cyclic, as claimed.

Now, we are almost there. It is well known (and not too hard to see) that  $SFTE$  is cyclic and that  $EF$  and  $ST$  intersect on  $QK$ , and thus  $L= EF \cap ST$ . Now, 
\[\measuredangle LRT = \measuredangle QRT = \measuredangle QHT = \measuredangle EHT = \measuredangle EFT \]
from which it is clear that  $LRFT$  is cyclic. Combining this with our previous claim, it follows that  $E,T,J,L$  are cyclic, as claimed.

<span style="color:#9a00ff">**Remark :**</span>Please note that in the above solution  $E$  and  $F$  are 'swapped' i.e  $E$  is the midpoint of  $AD$  and  $F$  is the midpoint of  $BC$ . This is clear in the diagram. This was the case in the original version of the problem which I received. Forgive any confusion caused.

