Let  $X,Y$  be the feet of  $E$  onto  $AB,AC$  and  $P,Q$  the foot of  $E$  onto  $BD$ ,  $DC$ . Let  $F$  be the foot of  $E$  onto  $AD$ . 

By extended sine law, it suffices to show that 
\[\frac{\sin{\measuredangle PXF}}{PF}=\frac{\sin{\measuredangle FYQ}}{FQ}\]
This is equivalent to showing that 
\[\frac{PF}{FQ}=\frac{\sin(\measuredangle PXE-\measuredangle FXE)}{\sin(\measuredangle FYE+\measuredangle EYQ)}\]
Yet note that by right angles from pedal property, that  $BXEP$ ,  $EYCQ$  and  $AXFEY$  are cyclic. Let  $(ABD)\cap BC= B'$ ,  $(ACD)\cap BC=C'$ . Then, by Bowtie, 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin(\measuredangle PXE-\measuredangle FXE)}{\sin(\measuredangle FYE+\measuredangle EYQ)}&=\frac{\sin(\measuredangle DBC-\measuredangle FAE)}{\sin(\measuredangle FAE+\measuredangle BCD)}
&=\frac{\sin(\measuredangle DAB'-\measuredangle FAE)}{\sin(\measuredangle FAE+\measuredangle C'AD)}
&=\frac{\sin{\measuredangle EAB'}}{\sin\measuredangle C'AE}
&=\frac{B'E}{C'E}\cdot\frac{AC'}{AB'}
\end{align*}
By Ratio Lemma. Yet because of angle bisectors and Bowtie, we get
\[\measuredangle DC'B'=\measuredangle DAC=\measuredangle BAD=\measuredangle C'B'D\]
Hence  $\triangle B'C'D$  is isosceles and  $E$  being the foot of  $D$  onto  $B'C'$  is the midpoint of  $B'C'$  hence  $\frac{B'E}{C'E}=1$ . 
Now, note that from right angles,  $EFPDQ$  is cyclic. Thus, using sine law and Bowtie on our circles,
\[\frac{FP}{FQ}=\frac{\sin \measuredangle FQP}{\sin\measuredangle QPF}=\frac{\sin \measuredangle FDP}{\sin\measuredangle QDF}=\frac{\sin\measuredangle AB'C'}{\sin\measuredangle B'C'A}\]
but the latter is simply  $\frac{AC'}{AB'}$  by sine law.
Hence, 
\[\frac{B'E}{C'E}\cdot\frac{AC'}{AB'} = 1\cdot \frac{FP}{FQ}=\frac{FP}{FQ}\]
as desired.

