Suppose that $f(x)=x^{2}-\frac{119}{3} x+\frac{1178}{3}=(x-19)\left(x-\frac{62}{3}\right) $ It's easy to find that $ f(19)=0, f(20)=-\frac{2}{3}, f(21)=\frac{2}{3} $ And when $ x \leq 18 $  or  $x \geq 22 $  , f(x)>0 . 
So \begin{align*}\sum_{i=1}^{100} f(i) a_{i} 
&\geq f(19) a_{19}+f(20) a_{20}+f(21) a_{21} 
&=\frac{2}{3}\left(a_{21}-a_{20}\right) \geq 0\end{align*}
On the other hand,
\begin{align*}\sum_{i=1}^{100} f(i) a_{i} 
 &=\sum_{i=1}^{100} i^{2} a_{i}-\frac{119}{3} \sum_{i=1}^{100} i a_{i}+\frac{1178}{3} \sum_{i=1}^{100} a_{i}
&=\sum_{i=1}^{100} i^{2} a_{i}-\frac{119}{3} \times 2022+\frac{1178}{3} \times 100 
& =\sum_{i=1}^{100} i^{2} a_{i}-40939 \frac{1}{3} \end{align*}
Also  $ \sum_{i=1}^{100} i^{2} a_{i} $  is an integer, So $\sum_{i=1}^{100} i^{2} a_{i} \geq\left\lceil 40939 \frac{1}{3}\right\rceil=40940 $ Then we are done. $\blacksquare$

The number of digits of  $a,b,c$  can be either 2, 2, 4, respectively, or 2, 3, 3, respectively. There are seven possible of combinations of the three leading digits:  $(2,2,4), (2,2,8), (2,2,9), (2,4,8), (2,4,9), (2,8,9), (4,8,9)$ .

When the number of digits of  $a,b,c$  are 2, 2, 4, respectively:

Consider choosing the leading digits  $(2,2,4)$ . If the two  $2$ 's are the leading digits of the 2-digit numbers, permute the remaining digits  $0,0,0,8,9$  and be sure to exclude cases when  $a=b=20$ :  $5!/3! - 3! = 14$ . Among the  $14$  cases, half of them is cases when  $a<b$  and the other half is cases when  $a>b$ . Therefore, we need to divide  $14$  by  $2$ . In conclusion, when the  $2$  is the leading digit of both  $a$  and  $b$ , there are  $7$  cases. When one of the  $2$ 's is the leading digit of  $a$  and  $4$  is the leading digit of  $b$ , then there are  $5!/3! = 20$ . Finally, when choosing (2,2,4), there are  $27$  cases.

Similarly, if we choose  $(2,2,8)$  or  $(2,2,9)$ , there will be  $27$  cases each.

If we choose  $(2,4,8)$ , then we choose one of them to be the leading digit of  $c$ , and then permute  $0,0,0,2,9$ :  $\textstyle \binom31\cdot 5!/3! = 60$  cases. Similarly, if we choose  $(2,4,9)$  or  $(2,8,9)$ , there will be  $60$  cases each.

If we choose  $(4,8,9)$ , then we choose one of them to be the leading digit of  $c$ , and then permute  $0,0,0,2,2$ :  $\textstyle \binom31 \tfrac{5!}{3!2!} = 30$  cases.

Therefore, when the number of digits of  $a,b,c$  are  $2,2,4$ , respectively, there are  $27+27+27+60+60+60+30 = 291$  cases.


When the number of digits of  $a,b,c$  are 2, 2, 3, respectively:

Consider choosing  $(2,2,4)$ . If the two  $2$ 's are leading both of the 3-digit numbers, then permute  $0,0,0,8,9$ , and finally divide by  $2$  as only half of the cases are actually  $b<c$ :  $\tfrac12\cdot(5!/3!) = 10$  cases. If a  $2$  is leading  $a$ , then there are  $5!/3!=20$  cases. In sum, there are  $30$  cases for  $(2,2,4)$ —and  $(2,2,8), (2,2,9)$  as well.

If we choose  $(2,4,8)$  (or  $(2,4,9), (2,8,9)$ ), choose one of them to be the leading digit for  $a$ , and then permute the remaining numbers:  $\textstyle \binom31 5!/3! = 60$  cases each.

If we choose  $(4,8,9)$ , choose one of them to be the leading digit for  $a$ , and then permute  $0,0,0,2,2$ :  $\textstyle \binom31\tfrac{5!}{3!2!} = 30$ Therefore, when the number of digits of  $a,b,c$  are 2, 2, 3, respectively, there are  $30+30+30+60+60+60+30 = 300$  cases.

At last, the answer is  $291+300 = \boxed{591}$ . The answer can be verified by the following C++ code:

