The answer is  $3, 10\nmid n$ , and the necessity proof is trivial. For sufficiency, suppose that  $n = p^aq$ , where  $p\in \{2,5\}$  and  $q$  is relatively prime to  $2$ ,  $3$ , and  $5$ . Also, let  $b$  be the order of  $10$  modulo  $q$ .**Lemma 1.** There exists an  $a$ -digit number  $P_a$  which is divisible by  $p^a$  and contains no  $0$ 's in its decimal representation.

*Proof.* We construct inductively. The base case follows by taking  $P_0$  the empty string. Suppose, then, that  $P_k$  exists. Now,  $$ 10^kx + P_k \equiv 0 \pmod{p^{k+1}} \iff \left(\frac{10}{p}\right)^k x + \frac{P_k}{p^k} \equiv 0 \pmod{p}, $$  which, by the fact that  $\tfrac{10}{p}$  is relatively prime to  $p$ , must have a solution. One such solution, call it  $x_0$ , must be in  $\{1, \ldots, 9\}$ , so we can set  $P_{k + 1} := \overline{x_0 P_k}$ . This completes the inductive step.  $\blacksquare$ **Lemma 2.** For any residue class  $[j]$  modulo  $q$ , there exists a number  $Q_j$  which contains no  $0$ 's in its decimal representation, has digit count  $1\pmod{b}$ , and satisifies  $Q_j \equiv j \pmod{q}$ .

*Proof.* We claim that taking  $$ Q_j := \big(10^b + 10^{b - 1} + \ldots + 1\big)\big(10^{(j - 1)b} + 10^{(j - 2)b} + \ldots + 1\big) $$  works. To see this, note that the decimal representation contains only  $1$ 's and  $2$ 's, that  $jb < \log_{10} Q_j < jb + 1$ , and that  $$ Q_j \equiv \left(10^b + \frac{10^b - 1}{9}\right)\cdot \underbrace{(1 + \ldots + 1)}_{j\text{ 1's}} \equiv \left(1 + \frac{0}{9}\right)\cdot j \equiv j \pmod{q}. $$   $\blacksquare$ Returning to the main problem, for  $q = 1$ , taking  $\overline{987654321P_a}$  obviously works. Otherwise, consider the recursion  $R_1:= \overline{Q_{-\frac{1}{9}}1}$  and  $R_{m + 1}:= \overline{R_mQ_{-10R_m-\frac{m+1}{9}}(m+1)}$  for  $m\in \{1, \ldots, 8\}$ . We claim that taking  $N := \overline{R_9 Q_{10R_9-\frac{P_a}{10^a}} P_{a}}$  when  $q\neq 1$  is sufficient.

First, we must show that  $n\mid N$ .  $p^a\mid N$  is trivial, so it suffices to show that  $q\mid N$ . Since  $P_a$  contains  $a$  digits and, by lemma 2,  $Q_{10R_{9}- \frac{P_a}{10^a}}$  has a digit count of  $tb + 1$  for some integer  $t$ , it follows that
\begin{align*}
N &\equiv R_9\cdot 10^a\cdot 10^{tb + 1} + Q_{10R_9 - \frac{P_a}{10^a}}\cdot 10^a + P_{a} &\pmod{q}
&\equiv R_1\cdot 10^{a + 1} + \left(10R_9 - \frac{P_a}{10^a}\right)\cdot 10^a + P_a &\pmod{q}
&\equiv 0 &\pmod{q}
\end{align*}
as desired.

Next, we must show the existence of digits  $1$  to  $9$  such that removal of any one of these digits leaves the string invariant modulo  $N$ . Here, we will show that to remove the digit  $i$ , we can do it from the end of the leftmost  $R_i$ : as a trivial consequence, we only need to show invariance modulo  $q$ . For  $i = 1$ , we have  $$ R_1 \equiv 10Q_{-\frac{1}{9}} + 1 \equiv - \frac{10}{9} + 1 \equiv -\frac{1}{9} \equiv Q_{-\frac{1}{9}}\pmod{q}. $$  And, for  $i \in \{2, \ldots, 9\}$ , invoke the fact that  $Q_{-10R_{i-1} - \frac{i}{9}}$  has  $sb + 1$  digits for some integer  $s$  to obtain
\begin{align*}
R_i &\equiv 10 \left(R_{i-1}\cdot 10^{sb + 1} + Q_{-10R_{i-1}-\frac{i}{9}}\right) + i &\pmod{q}
&\equiv 100R_{i-1} + 10Q_{-10R_{i-1}-\frac{ i}{9}} + i  &\pmod{q}
&\equiv 100R_{i-1} + 10\cdot \left(-10R_{i-1} - \frac{i}{9}\right) + i &\pmod{q}
&\equiv -\frac{i}{9} &\pmod{q}
&\equiv \overline{R_{i-1}Q_{-10R_{i-1} - \frac{i}{9}}} &\pmod{q}
\end{align*} $\blacksquare$

We claim that both the given ratios are equal to   $$ \dfrac{EC}{EB} \cdot \dfrac{BG}{GC}. $$   Indeed, by the Law of Sines in  $\Delta AHE$ , we have   $$ AH = \dfrac{EH \sin HEA}{\sin EAH}. $$   By the Law of Sines in  $\Delta HEF$ , we have   $$ HF = \dfrac{EH \sin FEH}{\sin EFH} $$   Combined with angle chasing in cyclic quadrilaterals, it follows that \begin{align*} 
\dfrac{AH}{HF} &= \frac{\sin HEA \sin EFH}{\sin EAH \sin FEH} 
&= \dfrac{\sin BEG \sin EBC}{\sin ECB \sin GEC} 
&= \dfrac{EC}{EB} \cdot \dfrac{\sin BEG}{\sin GEC} 
&= \dfrac{EC}{EB} \cdot \dfrac{\sin (90^{\circ} - GEC)}{\sin GEC} 
&= \dfrac{EC}{EB} \cdot \cot GEC 
&= \dfrac{EC}{EB} \cdot \cot GBC 
&= \dfrac{EC}{EB} \cdot \dfrac{GB}{GC}  
\end{align*}
as desired. Then, in right triangles  $\Delta EAC$  and  $\Delta GDC$ , we have   $$ \begin{cases} AC = \frac{EC}{\sin EAC}  CD = \frac{GC}{\sin GDC} \end{cases} $$   It follows that \begin{align*}
\dfrac{AC}{CD} &= \dfrac{EC \sin GDC}{GC \sin EAC} 
&= \dfrac{EC \sin BCG}{GC \sin ECB} 
&= \dfrac{EC \cdot \dfrac{BG}{BC}}{GC \cdot \dfrac{BE}{BC}} 
&= \dfrac{EC}{EB} \cdot \dfrac{BG}{GC}
\end{align*}
which leads to the desired condition.  $\blacksquare$

