So cute :) First, the statement is altogether wrong; there are **not**  $n$ **permutations** $P_1,P_2,\ldots,P_n$ , each being an arbitrary permutation of  $\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$ , since  $\dfrac {1} {P_i + P_{i+1}}$  makes no sense as a number, but rather  $n$  **numbers**  $P_1,P_2,\ldots,P_n$ , being an arbitrary**permutation**of  $\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$ . Second, the proof is quite nice, up to a patently reversed inequality; the right form is
\begin{align*}
\sum^{n-1}_{i=1} \frac{1}{P_i + P_{i+1}}&>\frac{(n-1)^2}{2(P_1+P_2+\cdots+P_n)-P_1-P_n}
&=\frac{(n-1)^2}{2(1+2+\cdots+n)-P_1-P_n}
&=\frac{(n-1)^2}{n(n+1)-P_1-P_n}
&\ge\frac{(n-1)^2}{n(n+1)-1-2}
&=\frac{(n-1)^2}{n^2+n-3}
&>\frac{(n-1)^2}{n^2+n-2}
&=\frac{n-1}{n+2}.\end{align*}

Most likely the best such permutation is  $1,n,2,n-1,\ldots, \lfloor n/2\rfloor +1$ .

