Let  $y_i$  be the partial sum of the first  $i$  terms in  $\{x_n\}$ . Then, for the lower bound, note that   $$ S = \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{y_i-y_{i-1}}{\sqrt{1+y_{i-1}} \cdot \sqrt{1-y_{i-1}}} = \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{y_i-y_{i-1}}{\sqrt{1-y_{i-1}^2}} \geq \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{y_i - y_{i-1}}1 = y_n = y_0 = 1. $$   For the RHS bound, let  $y_i = \sin \theta_i$  for some  $0=\theta_0<\theta_1<\cdots<\theta_n = \frac{\pi}2$ . Then, 
\begin{align*}
S &= \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{\sin \theta_i - \sin \theta_{i-1}}{\cos \theta_{i-1}} 
&= \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{2\cos \frac{\theta_i + \theta_{i-1}}2 \sin \frac{\theta_i-\theta_{i-1}}2}{\cos \theta_{i-1}} 
&< \sum_{i=1}^n 2\sin \frac{\theta_i - \theta_{i-1}}2 
&< \sum_{i=1}^n 2 \cdot \frac{\theta_i - \theta_{i-1}}2 = \theta_n - \theta_0 =\frac{\pi}2
\end{align*}
because  $\cos$  is strictly decreasing on  $\left[0, \frac{\pi}2\right]$  and  $\sin x < x$  for any angle in that interval. We are done.

