solution_md:
The "innovated degree" is usually known as the number of left-to-right maxima of the permutation.  A permutation of  $\{1,\ldots,n\}$  has exactly two left-to-right maxima if and only if its first entry  $k$  satisfies  $k<n$  and all entries in the permutation between (in position)  $k$  and  $n$  have value less than  $k$ .  Thus, the number of permutations with exactly two left-to-right maxima and first entry  $k$  is \begin{align*}
\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \binom{k-1}{j}j!(n-j-2)!
&= (k-1)!(n-k-1)!\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \binom{n-j-2}{n-k-1} 
&= (k-1)!(n-k-1)!\binom{n-1}{n-k}
&= \frac{(n-1)!}{n-k}
\end{align*} (possibly there was a cleverer way to see this).  Consequently, the average in question is (canceling common factors) \[
\frac{\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\frac{k}{n-k}}{\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\frac{1}{n-k}}=n-\frac{n-1}{H_{n-1}}.\]

