We first consider when  $abcd = 0:$ \begin{enumerate}
	\item If  $a = 0,$  we have the equation  $3^b - 5^c7^d = 1.$  However, the LHS is even.
	\item If  $b = 0,$   $2^a - 1 = 5^c 7 ^d.$  For  $a > 4,$  by Zsigmondy, we have that there exists a prime that isn't 5 or 7 that divides the left hand side. For  $a \leq4,$   $a = 3$  is the only solve.  $\boxed{(3, 0, 0, 1)}.$ 	\item If  $c = 0,$   $2^a3^b = 1 + 7^d.$  Note that  $3\not\mid1 + 7^d.$  For  $d > 1,$  we have that there exists prime that isn't 2 that divides  $1+7^d.$  For  $d\leq1,$  the non-repeat solve is  $\boxed{(1, 0, 0, 0)}$ 	\item If  $d = 0,$   $2^a3^b = 1 + 5^d.$  Hence, for  $d > 1,$  there exists a prime that isn't 2 or 3 that divides  $1 + 5^d.$  The only unique solution for  $d\leq1$  is  $\boxed{(1, 1, 1, 0)}$ \end{enumerate}

