I claim that all solutions in nonnegative integers to the given Diophantine equation are   $$ \boxed{(1, 0, 0, 0), (1, 1, 1, 0), (3, 0, 0, 1), (2, 2, 1, 1)}. $$   It is easy to check that they work, so it remains to show that they are the only solutions. First, I will consider the cases when one or more of  $a,b,c,$  or  $d$  equals  $0.$  

If  $a=0,$  then we have   $$ 3^b-5^c7^d=1 \implies 3^b-1=5^c7^d. $$   For  $0 \le b \le 6,$  it is easy to see that there are no solutions. Therefore, since   $$ 5 \mid 3^4-1, 7 \mid 3^6-1, $$   we know by Zsigmondy's Theorem that for all  $b>6,$  there exists some prime other than  $5$  or  $7$  that divides  $3^b-1,$  contradiction. 

If  $b=0,$  then we have   $$ 2^a-5^c7^d=1 \implies 2^a-1=5^c7^d. $$   For  $0 \le a \le 4,$  it is easy to check that  $(1,0,0,0)$  and  $(3,0,0,1)$  are the only solutions. Therefore, since   $$ 7 \mid 2^3-1, 5 \mid 2^4-1, $$   we know by Zsigmondy's Theorem that for all  $a>4,$  there exists some prime other than  $5$  or  $7$  that divides  $2^a-1,$  contradiction. 

If  $c=0,$  then we have   $$ 2^a3^b-7^d=1 \implies 7^d+1=2^a3^b. $$   Taking both sides modulo  $3,$  we must have  $b=0,$  reducing it to the previous case. 

If  $d=0,$  then we have   $$ 2^a3^b-5^c=1 \implies 5^c+1=2^a3^b. $$   For  $0 \le c \le 1,$  we have the solutions  $(1, 0, 0, 0)$  and  $(1,1,1,0).$  Therefore, since   $$ 2 \mid 5^1+1, 3 \mid 5^1+1, $$   we know by Zsigmondy's Theorem that for all  $c>1,$  there exists some prime other than  $2$  or  $3$  that divides  $5^c+1,$  contradiction. 

Now, assume that  $a, b, c,$  and  $d$  are all positive. Taking both sides modulo  $3,$  we get   $$ (-1)^c \equiv -1 \pmod{3}, $$   so  $c$  must be odd. Then, taking both sides modulo  $8,$  we get   $$ 5^c(-1)^d \equiv -1 \pmod{8}. $$   However, for odd  $c,$  we have  $5^c \equiv 5 \pmod{8},$  contradiction. Therefore, we must have  $a=1$  or  $a=2.$  

If  $a=1,$  then we have   $$ 2 \cdot 3^b = 5^c7^d+1. $$   Taking both sides modulo  $5,$  we get
\begin{align*}
    2 \cdot 3^b \equiv 1 \pmod{5} &\implies 3^b \equiv 3 \pmod{5} 
    &\implies b \equiv 1 \pmod{4},
\end{align*}
so  $b$  must be odd. However, taking both sides modulo  $7,$  we get
\begin{align*}
    2 \cdot 3^b \equiv 1 \pmod{7} &\implies 2 \cdot 3^b \equiv 4 \pmod{7} 
    &\implies b \equiv 4 \pmod{6},
\end{align*}
so  $b$  must be even, contradiction. Thus,  $a=1$  yields no solutions. 

If  $a=2,$  then we have   $$ 4 \cdot 3^b - 5^c7^d=1 \implies 4 \cdot 3^b=5^c7^d+1. $$   Taking both sides modulo  $5,$  we get
\begin{align*}
    4 \cdot 3^b \equiv 1 \pmod{5} &\implies 3^b \equiv 4 \pmod{5} 
    &\implies b \equiv 2 \pmod{4},
\end{align*}
i.e.  $b$  is even. Then, by difference of squares, we have
\begin{align*}
    4 \cdot 3^b-1 = 5^c7^d &\implies (2 \cdot 3^{b/2}-1)(2 \cdot 3^{b/2}+1)=5^c7^d 
    &\implies 2 \cdot 3^{b/2}-1 = 5^{c'}7^{d'},
\end{align*}
for some nonnegative integers  $c'$  and  $d'.$  We already know that this has no solutions in positive integers, so either  $c'=0$  or  $d'=0.$  The former case fails by taking both sides modulo  $3,$  so we have   $$ 5^{c'}+1=2 \cdot 3^{b/2}. $$   For  $0 \le c' \le 1,$  the only solution in which  $b$  is a positive integer is  $(b, c')=(2, 1).$  Then, since   $$ 2 \mid 5^1+1, 3 \mid 5^1+1, $$   we know by Zsigmondy's Theorem that for all  $c'>1,$  there exists a prime other than  $2$  or  $3$  dividing  $5^c+1,$  contradiction. Thus,  $b$  must be equal to  $2,$  so  $(c,d)$  must be  $(1,1),$  giving the solution  $(2,2,1,1).$  

Since we have exhausted all cases, we can indeed conclude that the only solutions in nonnegative integers to the given Diophantine equation are the claimed ones.  $\square$

