Before proceeding, we perform some preliminary calculations.  First, compute  $\angle AOB = 2\angle C = 60^\circ$ , so  $\triangle AOB$  is equilateral.  This means
\[
AD = AE = AB = AO = BO,
\]
so  $\triangle AOD$  and  $\triangle BOE$  are both isosceles.  Second, because  $I$  is the incenter of  $\triangle ABC$ , lines  $AI$  and  $BI$  bisect angles  $\angle CAB$  and  $\angle CBA$ , respectively.  Combined with the equality condition  $AD=BE=AB$ , we deduce 
\[
\triangle AID\cong \triangle AIB\cong\triangle EIB.\qquad(*)
\]
------
We now proceed with the problem by first tackling part (a).  The crucial claim is that  $O$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle DIE$ .  To prove this, let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{DO}$ , and let  $P$  be the intersection point of  $AM$  with  $BC$ .  Because  $\triangle ADO$  is isosceles, line  $AMP$  is perpendicular to  $DO$ .  Let  $\alpha := \angle IAB$ .  Then  $\angle CAB = 2\alpha$ , so
\[
\angle DAM = \frac12\angle DAO = \frac12(2\alpha - 60^\circ) = \alpha - 30^\circ.
\]
Combining this with  $\angle C = 30^\circ$ , it follows that 
\[
\angle APB = \angle ACP + \angle CAP = \alpha.
\]
Because  $\angle IEB = \alpha$  as well, we deduce  $AP\parallel IE$ , so  $DO\perp IE$ .  Similarly,  $EO\perp DI$ , so  $O$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle DIE$  and  $IO\perp DE$ .  This proves part (a).
